---
name: beam-message-rail-federation
description: Build a message rail — a microservice implementing IFederatedMessageRail that delivers a campaign's push, email, or in-game messages and reports a per-recipient funnel result.
---

# Build a Message Rail Federation

A **message rail** is the last mile of Beamable outreach. A campaign send node hands the
platform a list of recipients; the platform stages, coalesces and batches them, then calls
**your microservice** to do the actual delivery. Beamable ships no rail implementation — every
rail is a customer microservice implementing `IFederatedMessageRail<T>`.

Your service owns exactly two things: **talking to a provider** (APNs, an ESP, an internal
mailbox) and **reporting truthfully what happened to each recipient**. Everything else —
batching, retries, back-off, dedup, funnel counting — is the platform's, and all of it is
driven by the per-recipient result you return.

> This skill covers the message-rail contract only. For creating/running/deploying a
> microservice in general, load `beam-manage-service`. For an end-to-end federation example of
> a different kind, load `beam-microservice-examples`. For publishing the service as part of a
> bundle, load `beam-bundles`.

## Prerequisites

- A `.beamable` workspace (`beam init` — see `beam-initialize-project`).
- The channel you are delivering on, and its provider credentials, decided up front. The
  federation id names the **channel** (`push`, `email`, `sms`), not the vendor — swapping
  Postmark for SendGrid must not change the id, because producers target the id.

## Steps

### 1. Create the microservice

```
beam_exec("project new service <Name>MessageRailService -q")
```
{{ if commands["project new service"] }}
#### `beam project new service` options
| Option | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
{{ for opt in commands["project new service"].options }}`{{ opt.name }}` | {{ opt.type }} | {{ opt.description }}
{{ end }}{{ end }}

If the rail needs its own database (push does, for device tokens; email and in-game usually do
not — player consent belongs in stats):

```
beam_exec("project new storage <Name>RailStorage -q")
beam_exec("project deps add <Name>MessageRailService <Name>RailStorage")
```

### 2. Declare the federation id

One class, in the same file and namespace as the service. The const **is** the `federationId`
producers target and the path segment the platform POSTs to, so it must be stable forever.

```csharp
[FederationId(Id)]
public class SmsRailId : IFederationId
{
    public const string Id = "sms";
}
```

The id must start with a letter and contain only letters, digits or `_` — the source generator
rejects anything else. `my-rail` is not valid; `my_rail` is.

### 3. Implement the interface — all five methods

```csharp
[Microservice("SmsMessageRailService")]
public class SmsMessageRailService : Microservice, IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>
{
    async Promise<MessageRailSendResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.SendMessage(
        MessageRailRecipient recipient, MessageRailPayload payload)
        => await DeliverBatch(new List<MessageRailRecipient> { recipient }, payload);

    async Promise<MessageRailSendResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.SendMessageBatch(
        List<MessageRailRecipient> recipients, MessageRailPayload payload)
        => await DeliverBatch(recipients, payload);

    async Promise<MessageRailRegistrationResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.RegisterUserWithMessageRail(
        string playerId, Dictionary<string, string> registrationData) { /* opt in */ }

    async Promise<MessageRailRegistrationResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.UnregisterUserWithMessageRail(
        string playerId) { /* opt out */ }

    async Promise<MessageRailConfigStatus> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.CheckMessageRailConfig()
        { /* cheap preflight, step 6 */ }
}
```

Three rules that are easy to get wrong and fail silently:

- **Explicit interface implementations.** That is the convention every shipped rail follows.
- **Never put `[Callable]` on a federation method.** The route mounting skips any federation
  method carrying it, so the rail registers and then is simply never reachable.
- **`SendMessage` always delegates to the batch path.** The platform only ever dispatches
  `SendMessageBatch`; the singular form exists for completeness. Give it a one-element list and
  keep all logic in one private `DeliverBatch`.

Registration is automatic — reflection finds the interface at startup and self-registers the
federation. There is no CLI command for it (`beam federation add` is obsolete and throws).

### 4. Return a truthful per-recipient result

This is the whole contract. The platform reconciles what you return against what it sent.

```csharp
var response = new MessageRailSendResponse();
response.@params["provider"] = "twilio";
response.maxBatchSize = 500;              // your provider's cap; 0 = unlimited

response.sentPlayers.Add(gamerTag.ToString());
response.sentPayloadFed[gamerTag.ToString()] = payload.extraDataFed;

response.errorPlayersWithStatus.Add(new MessageRailErrorPlayerStatus
{
    playerId = gamerTag.ToString(),
    status = "network",
    message = "provider timed out",
    retriable = true,
});
```

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Report **every** input player exactly once across `sentPlayers` and `errorPlayersWithStatus` | A player reported in neither got no signal, so the platform retries them silently — an invisible duplicate-send risk |
| Classify each failure retriable-or-terminal **where you detect it** | Retriable keeps the recipient pending for another attempt; terminal stops them. Guessing later loses the reason |
| Stamp `params["delivery"] = "durable"` if no receipt will ever follow | Without it the `Delivered` funnel stage is structurally zero forever. Stamp it **once at construction**, not on individual return paths |
| Set `maxBatchSize` to your provider's cap and return the overflow as `"over-capacity"` + `retriable = true` | The platform caches the limit and right-sizes later pages instead of re-failing every round |
| **Never let an exception escape `DeliverBatch`** | An escaped exception discards outcomes already earned and the whole batch redelivers |

Conventional `status` strings (free-form, but stay consistent with the shipped rails):

| status | retriable | means |
|---|---|---|
| `network` | true | 5xx, timeout, transport, a failed credential read |
| `over-capacity` | true | overflow past your `maxBatchSize` — use `MessageRailContract.OverCapacityStatus` |
| `notConfigured` | true when the credential store was unreadable, false when credentials are present but rejected | cannot deliver right now |
| `invalid` | false | bad payload, no address on file, 4xx, a dead device token |
| `optedOut` | false | the player has not consented on this rail |
| `unknown` | false | nothing to deliver to (no registered device) |

Order the work so a batch-wide problem fails everyone identically: validate payload and
credentials first, then per-recipient gates (consent, address), then one provider call per page.

### 5. Carry the attribution and the tracking pixel

Both come per-recipient on `MessageRailRecipient` and both are how engagement gets attributed
back. Index them before you start rendering:

```csharp
var outreachByTag = new Dictionary<long, string>();
var pixelByTag = new Dictionary<long, string>();
foreach (var r in recipients)
{
    outreachByTag[r.gamerTag] = r.outreachId;
    pixelByTag[r.gamerTag] = r.openTrackingUrl;
}
```

- **`outreachId`** — embed it in the rendered message or deep link under
  `MessageRailContract.OutreachKey` (`beam_outreach`). It is the per-recipient join key; without
  it an Opened or Clicked event cannot attribute to anyone. Carry the payload's
  `analyticsTrackRef` alongside it as `trackId`, or the campaign drops the stage for having no
  track ref.
- **`openTrackingUrl`** — render it verbatim as a 1x1 image, and render **nothing at all** when
  it is empty. Empty means tracking is off; a broken image in an inbox is worse than a missing
  metric.

```csharp
if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(pixelUrl))
{
    var pixel = $"<img src=\"{HtmlEscape(pixelUrl)}\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\">";
    var closing = body.LastIndexOf("</body>", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
    body = closing >= 0 ? body.Insert(closing, pixel) : body + pixel;
}
```

**Never construct that URL yourself.** It carries a platform-signed token including the
recipient's gamer tag, which the campaign funnel needs to resolve an account. A hand-built copy
cannot be signed and drops that field, which makes every open it records invisible.

Do not add a hidden-image style, and turn your provider's own open/click tracking **off** — it
will otherwise inject a second pixel and rewrite your call-to-action href.

A push rail can ignore `openTrackingUrl` entirely: the handset reports Opened by echoing
`beam_outreach`. Email has no handset, which is why the pixel is its only signal.

### 6. Answer the publish-time preflight

`CheckMessageRailConfig()` is consulted **before** a campaign targeting your rail is published,
so it must be cheap — read your own configuration, make no provider round-trip.

```csharp
return new MessageRailConfigStatus
{
    configured = false,
    missingKeys = new List<string> { "twilio_sms/account_sid", "twilio_sms/auth_token" },
    message = "Set the Twilio credentials in Realm Secrets.",
};
```

Three states, and the difference between the last two decides whether someone can ship:

| State | Fields | Platform behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| OK | `configured = true` | publish proceeds |
| MISSING | `configured = false`, `missingKeys` populated | **blocks** the publish |
| INVALID | `configured = false`, no missing keys, `invalidReason` set | **blocks** the publish |
| UNKNOWN | `readable = false` | **never blocks** — an unreadable credential store is an outage, not a misconfiguration |

List **every** missing key, fully qualified as an operator would find it in the credential
store. Reporting one at a time turns a single setup into as many round-trips as there are keys.
`readable` defaults to `true` so an older build that never sets it reads as a meaningful answer.

### 7. Read credentials and consent

**Credentials — Realm Secrets.** Request the raw path rather than the generated client, which
URL-escapes the `/` inside the key and turns it into a 400:

```csharp
private async Task<string> TryGetRealmSecret(string key)
{
    try
    {
        return await Requester.Request<string>(
            Method.GET,
            $"/api/customers/{Context.Cid}/realms/{Context.Pid}/secrets/values/{key}",
            parser: json =>
            {
                using var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
                return doc.RootElement.TryGetProperty("value", out var v) &&
                       v.ValueKind == JsonValueKind.String ? v.GetString() : null;
            });
    }
    catch (RequesterException ex) when (ex.Status == 404)
    {
        return null;   // not set — a missing key, not a failure
    }
}
```

Swallow **only** the 404. Letting every other failure propagate is what lets you tell MISSING
apart from UNKNOWN in step 6.

**Consent — stats.** Opt-in belongs in a public game stat so the roster is searchable:

```csharp
await Services.Stats.SetStat(StatsDomainType.Game, StatsAccessType.Public,
    playerId, "sms_delivery_enabled", enabled ? "true" : "false");
```

Read it as `string.Equals(raw?.Trim(), "true", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)` — an absent
stat must mean blocked, never allowed. Refusing an opt-in (no phone number on file, say) is
`success = false` on a 200, never an exception.

### 8. Register it, run it, verify it

Add the service (and any storage) to your bundle manifest's `components` list — see
`beam-bundles`. Then:

```
beam_exec("project run --ids <Name>MessageRailService")
```

Confirm the federation registered by checking the generated OpenAPI document contains the five
routes `<federationId>/SendMessage`, `SendMessageBatch`, `RegisterUserWithMessageRail`,
`UnregisterUserWithMessageRail` and `CheckMessageRailConfig`.

A staged send only delivers while the platform's message-rail runtime is running. If sends sit
in staging and your service is never called, that runtime — not your service — is what is
missing.

## Reference: the shape to copy

`Program.cs` is the stock one; only the type changes.

```csharp
public static async Task Main()
{
    await BeamServer
        .Create()
        .IncludeRoutes<SmsMessageRailService>(routePrefix: "")
        .RunForever();
}
```

The `.csproj` needs no message-rail-specific entries — the generated one is correct. Keep
`<BeamProjectType>service</BeamProjectType>`, and set `<BeamServiceGroup>` by hand if your group
name contains a hyphen, which `beam project group add` rejects.

## Pitfalls

- **`[Callable]` on a federation method** — routes never mount; the service looks healthy and is
  unreachable. The single most common cause of "my rail registered but is never called".
- **Stamping `params` on one return path** — a past bug set `delivery` only on the
  nobody-opted-in early return, so every real send reconciled as non-durable and `Delivered`
  stayed permanently zero.
- **Building the tracking URL yourself** — silently costs every open.
- **Rendering a pixel for an empty URL** — a broken image in the recipient's inbox.
- **Letting an exception escape `DeliverBatch`** — discards outcomes already earned.
- **Reporting a player in neither result list** — an invisible retry.
- **A provider's own open tracking left on** — a second pixel and a rewritten CTA link.
- **Changing the `[FederationId]` const after launch** — producers target that string; it is a
  breaking change, not a rename.

## Checklist

- [ ] `[FederationId]` const names the channel, valid identifier, stable
- [ ] Five explicit interface members, none marked `[Callable]`
- [ ] `SendMessage` delegates to the same `DeliverBatch` as `SendMessageBatch`
- [ ] Every input player appears exactly once in the response
- [ ] Failures classified retriable-or-terminal at the point of detection
- [ ] `params["delivery"] = "durable"` stamped once at construction, if no receipt follows
- [ ] `maxBatchSize` set; overflow returned as retriable `over-capacity`
- [ ] `beam_outreach` + `trackId` embedded in the rendered message
- [ ] `openTrackingUrl` rendered verbatim, nothing when empty, provider tracking off
- [ ] `CheckMessageRailConfig` is cheap, lists all missing keys, sets `readable = false` on a read failure
- [ ] Nothing throws out of `DeliverBatch`
- [ ] Service and storage listed in the bundle manifest `components`
